import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/McpTemperatures
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap

## The MCP package: a first server, what it answers (real requests, sent to it while the site is
## built), serving it over HTTP, what it speaks, its modules, who may ask, testing, and its status.
## Nothing here hydrates: the page is text and code, and its server's answers are the build's.
meta = {
	title: "MCP",
	description: "A Roc Solid app as an MCP server: its tools, resources and prompts, for AI agents. The protocol is plain Roc; an endpoint becomes a tool in a line, its schema the platform's.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "first", label: "A first server" },
		{ id: "answers", label: "What it answers" },
		{ id: "http", label: "Over HTTP" },
		{ id: "speaks", label: "What it speaks" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "access", label: "Who may ask" },
		{ id: "testing", label: "Testing a server" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Limits, for now" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<NoHydration>
		<PageHero
			kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/mcp</code>]}
			title="Your app's tools, for AI agents"
		>
			MCP, the Model Context Protocol, is how AI agents, and the apps they run in (Claude, an editor,
			the MCP Inspector), find out what an app can do, and do it. This package makes a Roc Solid app an MCP
			server: its tools, the data it lets agents read (resources), and messages they can start from
			(prompts). An endpoint the app already has becomes a tool in a line, and the JSON Schema of the
			tool's arguments is the one the platform writes for <code>/openapi.json</code>, from the same type.
		</PageHero>

		<Heading id="first">A first server</Heading>
		<p>
			A server is a record: a name, a version, and what it offers. This one converts temperatures. Its
			tool's handler is a Roc function from a record to a record: the arguments an agent sends are read
			into its type, and its answer goes back as JSON. A handler that says <code>Err</code> refuses, and
			the model reads why.
		</p>
		<Code title={Sources.mcp_server.file} source={Sources.mcp_server.rocx} />
		<p>
			The schemas here are written by hand, because this page's server has no HTTP side. Over HTTP,
			<code>McpHttp.tool</code> writes them from the handler's types (<a href="#http">below</a>). A
			resource is something an agent reads by its URI; a template is a family of them, whose URIs it
			matches (<code>{"{substance}"}</code> takes what the URI has there); a prompt makes messages from
			the arguments a person gives.
		</p>

		<Heading id="answers">What it answers</Heading>
		<p>
			This site is static files, so nothing here serves MCP. While the site was built, this page sent the
			server above these requests, as a client sends them, and shows what the package answered, as it
			answered it (indented to be read).
		</p>
		{...McpTemperatures.exchanges!({}).map_with_index(
			|exchange, index|
				<details class="mcp-exchange" open?={index == 0}>
					<summary>{exchange.title}</summary>
					<div class="mcp-pair">
						<Code title="Request" source={exchange.request} numbers={Bool.False} />
						<Code title="Answer" source={exchange.answer} numbers={Bool.False} />
					</div>
				</details>,
		)}

		<Heading id="http">Over HTTP</Heading>
		<p>
			The protocol is plain Roc, on no platform. Its HTTP side, <code>McpHttp</code>, is a module on the
			server platform, in the package's <code>server/</code> folder. It serves the MCP server at a path,
			behind the middleware that decides who may ask, and makes tools from what the app already has: an
			endpoint, a function of its own, a handler of its own.
		</p>
		<Code title="A sketch (this site serves no MCP): main.roc, and the server" source={Sources.mcp_http_sketch.rocx} />
		<TableWrap>
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>McpHttp.</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td><code>middleware(path, server)</code></td><td>the MCP server as a middleware: it answers <code>path</code>, and hands every other request on</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>respond!(server, request, context)</code></td><td>the answer to a request to the MCP route, for a route file's <code>respond!</code></td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>endpoint(Handlers.x({"{}"}), about, McpHttp.openapi({"{}"}))</code></td><td>an endpoint as a tool (<code>Generated/Handlers</code>', which has its handler, D-037): its body is the arguments, its answer the tool's, a refusal goes back to the model</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>function(Module.name!, about, McpHttp.openapi({"{}"}))</code></td><td>a function of the app's own as a tool, run in the MCP request, behind the MCP route's middleware</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>tool(about, handler!, McpHttp.openapi({"{}"}))</code></td><td>a handler of the app's own, its schemas from its types</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Caller(c)</code></td><td>what a handler is given: <code>{"{ request, context, progress! }"}</code>; <code>context</code> is what the middleware handed on, who is asking</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>About</code></td><td><code>{"{ name, description }"}</code>, and <code>title</code> and <code>tags</code> if wanted</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</TableWrap>
		<p>
			<code>{"McpHttp.openapi({})"}</code> is the platform's schema writers (<code>OpenApi</code>'s), given as
			a value, so that they meet the types where the call has them: one generic function can't both read a
			type with <code>Json</code> and describe it with <code>OpenApi</code>. A tool marked
			<code>.reporting()</code> may call <code>progress!</code>, and a client that asks to hear it gets the
			answer as a stream of server-sent events: each step as it is taken, then the answer.
		</p>
		<Callout kind="note" title="Copied in, for now">
			<p>
				A Roc package can name a platform only if it is the app's own, and an app is two programs, on two
				platforms. So until <code>rocx build</code> copies a package's server modules into the server's
				half (the proposal "Server-only modules in packages"), an app copies
				<code>server/McpHttp.roc</code> in by hand, and names the protocol's package <code>mcp</code> in
				its header.
			</p>
		</Callout>

		<Heading id="speaks">What it speaks</Heading>
		<p>
			MCP over Streamable HTTP: one endpoint, a JSON-RPC message a POST, answered with JSON (or with a
			stream of events, for a tool that reports its progress). Both eras of the protocol, on the same
			endpoint:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>2026-07-28</strong>, the current one, has no handshake. Each request says its version and the
				client's capabilities in its <code>_meta</code>, and its headers repeat them
				(<code>MCP-Protocol-Version</code>, <code>Mcp-Method</code>, <code>Mcp-Name</code>).
				<code>server/discover</code> says what the server offers. Lists say how long a client may keep
				them.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>2025-11-25, 2025-06-18 and 2025-03-26</strong> start with <code>initialize</code>, then
				<code>notifications/initialized</code>. The server keeps no session between requests, which these
				allow: it sends no <code>Mcp-Session-Id</code>.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<TableWrap>
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>A request</th><th>Is answered</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td>a notification (no id)</td><td>202, with nothing</td></tr>
					<tr><td>not one JSON-RPC message (not JSON, a batch, no method)</td><td>400, a JSON-RPC error</td></tr>
					<tr><td>a version the server doesn't speak; a header that doesn't match the body; <code>_meta</code> without what it needs</td><td>400, a JSON-RPC error</td></tr>
					<tr><td>no such method (current MCP)</td><td>404, a JSON-RPC error</td></tr>
					<tr><td>an unknown tool, a bad cursor, no such resource</td><td>200, a JSON-RPC error</td></tr>
					<tr><td>arguments that don't fit a tool, or a tool's refusal</td><td>200, a result the model reads (<code>isError</code>)</td></tr>
					<tr><td>from a page of another site (its <code>Origin</code>)</td><td>403</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>GET</code>, <code>DELETE</code></td><td>405: the server pushes nothing, and has no session to end</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</TableWrap>

		<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
		<p>
			Plain Roc, on no platform: it reads a request, checks it, and answers it or says what to run. It has
			no effects of its own: tools, resources and prompts are functions the app gives it.
			<code>roc test packages/mcp/main.roc</code> runs their <code>expect</code>s, the spec's example
			messages among them.
		</p>
		<TableWrap>
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td><code>Mcp</code></td><td><code>Mcp.Server(ctx)</code>, what a server offers; <code>handle!(server, incoming, ctx)</code>, a request's answer; <code>receive</code>, the same, pure: an answer, or what to run; <code>problems(server)</code>, for a test</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Tool</code></td><td><code>Tool.typed(name, description, schema, run!)</code>: arguments read into a Roc type, the answer written as JSON; <code>Tool.new</code>: any JSON in, content out; hints (<code>read_only</code>, <code>additive</code>, <code>idempotent</code>, <code>closed_world</code>), <code>titled</code>, <code>answers(schema)</code>, <code>reporting</code></td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Resource</code></td><td><code>Resource.text(uri, name, mime, read!)</code>, <code>Resource.new(uri, name, read!)</code>: what reading a URI gives; <code>Resource.contents</code>, text or bytes</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Template</code></td><td><code>Template.new(pattern, name, read!)</code>: a family of resources, read with the values its URI gave the template's names</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Prompt</code></td><td><code>Prompt.new(name, description, arguments, get!)</code>: messages from string arguments (<code>Prompt.argument</code>, <code>Prompt.optional</code>, <code>Prompt.user</code>, <code>Prompt.assistant</code>)</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Content</code></td><td>what a tool answers with: text, an image or a sound (bytes, sent as Base64), a link to a resource, a resource's contents</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Auth</code></td><td>the pieces of OAuth's answers, for the app's middleware (<a href="#access">below</a>)</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>JsonValue</code></td><td>any JSON, read a field at a time; <code>decode</code> into a Roc type and <code>encode</code> from one; a number keeps the text it came as</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Rpc</code></td><td>JSON-RPC 2.0: <code>read</code> one message; write a <code>result</code>, an <code>error</code> or a <code>notification</code></td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</TableWrap>
		<p>
			<code>Mcp.Server(ctx)</code> needs only <code>name</code> and <code>version</code>; the rest have
			defaults: <code>title</code>, <code>instructions</code> (for the model: how the tools go together),
			<code>tools</code>, <code>resources</code>, <code>templates</code>, <code>prompts</code>,
			<code>page_size</code> (100 to a list's page), <code>ttl_ms</code> (how long a client may keep a list)
			and <code>origins</code> (pages of other sites whose requests are answered). There are no components:
			an agent is the package's reader, not a person.
		</p>

		<Heading id="access">Who may ask</Heading>
		<p>
			The middleware in front of the MCP route decides, as for any route: a session cookie, an API key, an
			OAuth access token. MCP's own way is OAuth: a client without a good token is answered 401, with where
			to learn how to get one. <code>Auth</code> has those answers: <code>bearer</code> (the token a request
			carries), <code>challenge</code> (401, and <code>WWW-Authenticate</code>), <code>insufficient</code>
			(403) and <code>metadata</code> (the document a server serves at
			<code>/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource</code>). Issuing tokens, an authorization server of the
			platform's own, is a proposal. A request a browser sends from a page of another site is refused, so a
			page can't reach a server on the reader's own computer.
		</p>

		<Heading id="testing">Testing a server</Heading>
		<p>
			<code>Mcp.receive</code> is pure, so an app's <code>expect</code>s can send its server requests and read
			the answers, with no network. <code>Mcp.problems</code> names what MCP won't take: a tool name that
			isn't 1 to 128 letters, digits, <code>_</code>, <code>-</code> and <code>.</code>; a name or a URI given
			twice; a schema that isn't JSON. The server on this page is tested so:
		</p>
		<Code title={Sources.mcp_test.file} source={Sources.mcp_test.rocx} />

		<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
		<p>Works today:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				The protocol, both eras: 137 <code>expect</code>s. And over HTTP, in an app built with
				<code>rocx build</code> on the server platform, with the official clients: the MCP Inspector's
				command line (2.16.0, which speaks 2025-11-25), and the TypeScript SDK's client (2.4.0) on 2026-07-28.
				Tools from an endpoint, a function and handlers, refusals, resources, a template, a prompt,
				and a tool's progress, streamed as events.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>Waits on a proposal (in <code>packages/mcp/PROPOSALS.md</code>):</p>
		<ul>
			<li>the HTTP side reaches an app by hand, until "Server-only modules in packages";</li>
			<li>
				a route file can't serve an MCP server whose tools are <code>Api</code>'s: <code>Api</code> imports
				every route file, endpoints or not, so the two would import each other. Today it is mounted as a
				middleware;
			</li>
			<li>
				a tag union in a tool's arguments is "any value" until <code>Api</code> lists the tags it found
				(<code>Api.tags</code>); and an endpoint's doc comment isn't its tool's description yet;
			</li>
			<li>an authorization server (OAuth) of the platform's own.</li>
		</ul>

		<Heading id="limits">Limits, for now</Heading>
		<ul>
			<li>
				No list-change notifications or resource subscriptions, no completions, no logging to the client,
				and no requests from the server to the client (sampling, asking the user).
			</li>
			<li>No sessions: nothing is kept between requests.</li>
			<li>
				Every field of a tool's arguments is required, as the platform's schemas say; an optional argument
				is a <code>Tool.new</code> tool's, which reads any JSON.
			</li>
			<li>A batch, a JSON array of messages, is refused: MCP has had none since 2025-06-18.</li>
		</ul>
		<details>
			<summary>This page's server, and the requests it was sent: <code>{Sources.mcp_demo.file}</code></summary>
			<Code title={Sources.mcp_demo.file} source={Sources.mcp_demo.rocx} />
		</details>
		<details>
			<summary>The HTTP side: <code>{Sources.mcp_http.file}</code></summary>
			<Code title={Sources.mcp_http.file} source={Sources.mcp_http.rocx} />
		</details>
	</NoHydration>

<style>
	/* A request and its answer, side by side where there is room. */
	.mcp-pair {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 22rem), 1fr));
		gap: var(--space-4);
		align-items: start;
	}

	/* <Code>'s figure is that component's, so reached with :global. */
	.mcp-pair :global(.code) {
		margin: 0;
	}
</style>
